


{"id":"51085c84-6244-4f64-b70e-518d3151a2e7","object":"chat.completion","created":1786446162,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_050\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_08\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_06\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 20,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 3,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_10\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 24,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_07\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 4,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task with baseline decisions, instance data, and intervention overrides. The task is to produce a repair. The baseline decisions are provided but they likely need to be adjusted because of the intervention perturbations. The baseline decisions: \nMISSION_01: UAV_02, depart_slot 6, path_id PATH_07, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_02: UAV_01, depart_slot 20, path_id PATH_06, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_03: UAV_03, depart_slot 3, path_id PATH_04, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_04: UAV_02, depart_slot 22, path_id PATH_02, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_05: UAV_01, depart_slot 24, path_id PATH_10, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_06: UAV_03, depart_slot 7, path_id PATH_01, charge_blocks 0\nMISSION_07: UAV_02, depart_slot 3, path_id PATH_09, charge_blocks 0\n\nWe need to check if these baseline decisions are still feasible given the interventions. The intervention includes:\n\n- initial_soc_overrides: UAV_03 initial SOC changed to 1.710895 kWh (from original 2.760895? Actually the instance had initial_soc_kwh for UAV_03: 2.760895, but override changes it to 1.710895). So UAV_03 starts with less energy.\n- corridor_capacity_overrides: COR_01 capacity 1 at slot 3.\n- closed_corridor_slots: COR_07 closed from slot 6 to 43, COR_04 closed from slot 6 to 17. So many slots are closed for those corridors.\n- There are also charger_capacity_overrides none, pad_capacity_overrides none, mission_window_overrides none.\n\nWe must respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order. The assignment_order_contract is \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". So each mission is pre-assigned to a specific UAV and must be executed in the given sequence_index order. The UAV assignments: MISSION_01 (seq 1) UAV_02; MISSION_02 (seq 0) UAV_01; MISSION_03 (seq 0) UAV_03; MISSION_04 (seq 2) UAV_02; MISSION_05 (seq 1) UAV_01; MISSION_06 (seq 1) UAV_03; MISSION_07 (seq 0) UAV_02. For each UAV, the missions must be flown in the order of sequence_index. So for UAV_02: seq0 MISSION_07, seq1 MISSION_01, seq2 MISSION_04. For UAV_01: seq0 MISSION_02, seq1 MISSION_05. For UAV_03: seq0 MISSION_03, seq1 MISSION_06.\n\nWe must ensure feasibility: \n- Each mission must depart at its release_slot or later, arrive by due_slot? Actually due_slot likely means the mission must be completed (arrival at destination) by that slot, but the wording \"due_slot\" is given. The baseline had depart_slot and arrival presumably depart_slot + travel_slots = arrival_slot? We need to ensure that the service flight is completed by the due_slot. The due_slot is the slot by which the mission must arrive at destination? The baseline mission MISSION_01 due 23, depart 6 with PATH_07 travel 2 slots, arrives at 8, which is well before 23. So due_slot is a deadline. So we can depart later as long as arrival <= due_slot.\n- Path compatibility: UAV must be compatible with path (compatible_layers match altitude layer, wind level <= max_wind_level). All paths given have compatible true.\n- Energy feasibility: UAV must have enough energy for incoming deadhead (if any) + service flight + reserve. Incoming deadhead: for each mission, there may be an incoming deadhead from expected_incoming_origin_vertiport? In this instance, all missions have incoming_deadhead: null. So no incoming deadhead leg. The mission starts from its origin_vertiport, but the UAV must be at that origin at the start. The UAV starts at start_vertiport (VP_07). For the first mission of each UAV, we need to check if the UAV needs to travel from its current location (which after previous mission is the destination of previous mission) to the origin of the next mission. Since sequence is fixed, we can compute the needed deadhead distance and energy. But the task says \"incoming deadhead\" is part of the normative SOC event order: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". For the first mission of a UAV, the incoming deadhead is from start_vertiport to mission origin. For subsequent missions, it's from previous mission's destination to current mission's origin. We need to compute that distance and energy. The baseline didn't show any deadhead details, but we must incorporate them. The deadhead paths? The instance doesn't provide explicit deadhead paths, but we can assume that the deadhead is a repositioning flight using the corridors? Probably there is a network. The corridors given are directed? They have from/to. We need to find possible deadhead paths between any two vertiports. The given corridors may not directly connect all pairs. For instance, from VP_07 to VP_01: COR_01, COR_03, COR_06. From VP_01 to VP_02: COR_04, COR_05, COR_07. From VP_02 to VP_04: COR_08. From VP_03 to VP_05: COR_10. From VP_07 to VP_03: COR_09. From VP_01 to VP_06: COR_02. Other connections? For deadheading, we need to move between these ports. The UAVs may need to use corridors for deadhead as well. We must consider the shortest available deadhead route, respecting closures and capacities? The deadhead contract is \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\". This suggests there is a registered deadhead path for each mission, but it says incoming_deadhead: null in the data. Maybe this means no incoming deadhead is needed because the UAV is already at the required origin for its first mission? Let's check UAV starting positions: all UAVs start at VP_07. UAV_02 first mission MISSION_07 origin VP_07, so no deadhead needed. UAV_03 first mission MISSION_03 origin VP_07, no deadhead. UAV_01 first mission MISSION_02 origin VP_07, no deadhead. So first missions have zero deadhead. For subsequent missions: UAV_02 after MISSION_07 destination VP_01, next MISSION_01 origin VP_01, so deadhead from VP_01 to VP_01 is zero. Then MISSION_01 destination VP_02, next MISSION_04 origin VP_02, zero deadhead. So UAV_02 has no deadhead legs. UAV_03: MISSION_03 destination VP_01, next MISSION_06 origin VP_01, zero deadhead. So no deadheading required for any mission. So incoming deadhead energy is zero for all missions. Good.\n\nThus energy consumption only includes service flight energy (the path chosen) and payload energy? The path energy given (energy_kwh) likely already includes payload? Let's see: The path energy is provided per path option, e.g., PATH_07 energy 0.167248. The UAV parameters: empty_energy_kwh_per_km 0.1, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg 0.026. The payload for all missions is 0.5 kg. For PATH_07 distance 1.04 km. Energy = empty_energy_per_km*distance + payload_energy_per_km_kg*distance*payload_kg = 0.1*1.04 + 0.026*1.04*0.5 = 0.104 + 0.01352 = 0.11752. But given energy is 0.167248. That's higher, maybe includes wind? UAV_02 has wind_energy_multiplier_per_level 0.065. Wind level for PATH_07 is 1. So energy multiplier? The path energy likely already includes wind effects. The instance says \"wind_energy_multiplier_per_level\": for each UAV. For UAV_02, wind multiplier is 0.065 per wind level. So for wind level 1, multiplier = 1 + 0.065 = 1.065? Actually the parameter says \"wind_energy_multiplier_per_level\": 0.065, so wind multiplier = 1 + wind_level * multiplier? Typically yes. So empty energy consumption = empty_energy_kwh_per_km * distance * (1 + wind_multiplier*wind_level). Similarly for payload. So PATH_07 energy_kwh = (0.145*1.04 + 0.012*1.04*0.5) * (1 + 0.065*1) = (0.1508 + 0.00624) * 1.065 = 0.15704 * 1.065 ≈ 0.167248. Yes. So the given energy_kwh values are correct per UAV type and path. We should use those values directly.\n\nReserve check: after deducting incoming deadhead (0) + service flight energy, the remaining energy must be >= UAV reserve_kwh. UAV_02 reserve 2.5, UAV_01 reserve 1.5, UAV_03 reserve 1.5.\n\nBattery capacity check: after post-service charging (charge_blocks) the SOC must not exceed battery capacity. For UAV_02 capacity 22, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95. For UAV_01 capacity 16, charge_per_block 1.2. For UAV_03 capacity 16, charge 1.2.\n\nCharging: charge_blocks can be 0 to max_charge_blocks (3). Charging occurs at destination after the turnaround time (turnaround_slots = 1). The charging takes place during slots. The charger capacity at the destination must be respected per slot. The chargers are limited: each vertiport has 2 chargers. The charging occupied slots: from after arrival + turnaround + maybe some duration? Charge_blocks means the number of charge blocks; each block might take 1 slot? Not explicitly stated, but \"charge_blocks\" likely corresponds to number of slots of charging, each adding charge_kwh_per_block. Usually in these tasks, each charge block takes one slot. So if charge_blocks = c, then the UAV occupies a charger for c slots starting at slot = arrival_slot + travel_slots (arrive) + turnaround_slots. But the exact order: service flight, reserve check, destination turnaround (1 slot), then post-service charge for c slots. So charging starts after turnaround. Charger capacity by slot: each vertiport has 2 chargers, so at any slot, at most 2 UAVs can be charging simultaneously. We must ensure the charging slots for each UAV don't conflict.\n\nCorridor capacity: each corridor has capacity 2 per slot, except overrides and closures. For each UAV, the service flight uses a path consisting of edge segments with corridor_id, duration_slots, offset_slot. The UAV occupies the corridor during the specified slots. We need to check that the corridor capacity is not exceeded. Also the closed_corridor_slots overrides: for COR_07 closed slots 6-43, so COR_07 cannot be used in those slots. For COR_04 closed slots 6-17. So any path that uses those corridors must not have their flight slots overlapping closed slots. The corridor capacity overrides: COR_01 capacity 1 at slot 3.\n\nPad capacity: each vertiport has pad capacity 2 for arrival/departure? Likely pad capacity applies to the UAV being at the vertiport (occupying a pad) during turnaround and charging. The order says \"destination turnaround -> post-service charge\" both at destination. So after arrival, the UAV occupies a pad for turnaround (1 slot) and then for charging? Actually pad capacity might apply to the aircraft being on the ground. Typically, a UAV at a vertiport uses a pad during the entire stay, including turnaround and charging. So from arrival until departure for next mission (or end), it occupies a pad. However, the baseline didn't seem to enforce pad capacity during charging, but we must respect it. In the task, pad_capacity_by_slot_default is 2 per vertiport. So at any slot, the number of UAVs at a vertiport (including those in turnaround and charging) cannot exceed pad capacity. We need to model this.\n\nDeparture slots: each mission departs at its depart_slot. That means the UAV takes off from the origin at that slot. For mission to be feasible, the UAV must be at origin before that slot, and pad capacity at origin must be available? Possibly also pad capacity at origin for departure? Likely the UAV occupies a pad at origin from its arrival (if it arrived there) until departure, plus charging etc. For first missions, they start at VP_07. UAVs start at VP_07 at slot 0? Their available_slot is 3, meaning they become available at slot 3? Fleet: each UAV has \"available_slot\": 3. So they cannot be used before slot 3. Also they start at start_vertiport VP_07, and initial SOC given. So the earliest departure for any mission is max(available_slot, release_slot). For MISSION_07 release 3, UAV_02 available 3, so depart >=3. Baseline depart 3. MISSION_03 release 3, UAV_03 available 3, depart 3 baseline. MISSION_02 release 20, UAV_01 available 3, so depart >=20, baseline 20. So these are fine.\n\nNow, check baseline feasibility given interventions. Let's evaluate each mission's path and timing, considering closures, capacity, energy.\n\nUAV_02 missions:\nMISSION_07 (seq0): origin VP_07, dest VP_01, path PATH_09 (travel 1 slot, energy 0.200697, corridor COR_01). Depart slot 3. Arrival slot = depart + travel = 4. Check corridor COR_01: in slot 3? PATH_09 edge_segments: corridor COR_01, duration_slots 1, offset_slot 0. So it uses COR_01 at slot 3. corridor_capacity_overrides: COR_01 capacity 1 at slot 3. So capacity is 1. Only this flight uses it? Other UAVs might also use COR_01 at slot 3. Check other missions: MISSION_03 also departs slot 3, path PATH_04 uses COR_01? PATH_04: corridor COR_01, duration_slots 2, offset_slot 0. So it uses COR_01 at slots 3 and 4. So both MISSION_07 and MISSION_03 use COR_01 at slot 3, but capacity is 1, so conflict! Thus baseline is infeasible due to corridor capacity override. We need to adjust depart slots or paths to avoid conflict.\n\nMISSION_03 (UAV_03) seq0: origin VP_07, dest VP_01, path PATH_04 (COR_01, 2 slots, energy 0.156537). Depart slot 3, arrival 5. That uses COR_01 at slot 3 and 4. With MISSION_07 using COR_01 at slot 3, conflict. So we need to shift one of them, or change path. MISSION_07 has only PATH_09 (COR_01, 1 slot). MISSION_03 has PATH_04 (COR_01, 2 slots) and PATH_05 (COR_06, 8 slots, wind 0, energy 1.356). PATH_05 uses COR_06, which has no closures, capacity 2. Could use PATH_05 for MISSION_03 to avoid COR_01 conflict. But PATH_05 travel 8 slots, so depart 3 -> arrival 11. Due_slot for MISSION_03 is 9, so arrival 11 would be late. So cannot use PATH_05 because arrival > due 9. So MISSION_03 must use PATH_04 (fast) or be delayed. If we delay MISSION_03 depart to after slot 3 when COR_01 capacity allows? COR_01 capacity override only at slot 3, otherwise capacity 2. So if we delay MISSION_03 depart to slot 4, arrival 6, still due 9 feasible. But then MISSION_07 could depart at 3 using COR_01 (capacity 1 okay if MISSION_03 not using slot 3). However, MISSION_07 depart slot 3 uses COR_01 alone. But we also need to check other constraints for MISSION_03: release_slot 3, so can depart later. So shifting MISSION_03 to depart 4 would work. But also check MISSION_06 (UAV_03 seq1) which follows. Baseline MISSION_06 depart 7 from VP_01 to VP_06. If MISSION_03 arrives at VP_01 at slot 6 (if depart 4 + travel 2), then turnaround 1 slot? After arrival at slot 6, turnaround 1 slot (slot 6?), actually order: arrive, reserve check, destination turnaround for 1 slot. Then post-service charge (0 blocks). So UAV_03 becomes available at VP_01 at slot 7? Turnaround takes slot 6, so ready for next mission after slot 6? For departure at slot 7, that's fine. So delaying MISSION_03 to depart 4 seems feasible. But we need to check energy: initial SOC for UAV_03 after override is 1.710895. MISSION_03 service energy 0.156537, reserve 1.5. So required: 0.156537 + 1.5 = 1.656537. Initial SOC 1.710895 > 1.656537, so OK. After mission, SOC = 1.710895 - 0.156537 = 1.554358. Reserve check: 1.554358 >= 1.5, OK. Then destination turnaround, no charging, SOC remains 1.554358 for next mission. MISSION_06 energy 0.104358, reserve 1.5. Required 1.604358 but SOC only 1.554358, so INFEASIBLE! So UAV_03 cannot do MISSION_03 then MISSION_06 without charging. So we need to charge between missions. MISSION_03 could charge after arriving at VP_01. Charge_blocks can be added. Max 3 blocks, each block adds 1.2 kWh. So if we charge, say 1 block (1.2), SOC becomes 1.554358 + 1.2 = 2.754358, but battery capacity is 16, so okay. Reserve check after mission was fine. Then for MISSION_06, need 0.104358 + 1.5 = 1.604358, and SOC 2.754358 >= 1.604358, ok. So charging helps. But charging takes time and charger capacity. Charger capacity at VP_01 is 2 chargers. MISSION_03 arrives at VP_01 at slot 6 (if depart 4 + travel 2). Turnaround 1 slot (slot 6). Then charging starts slot 7 for number of blocks. So if we charge 1 block, it would occupy slot 7. Then UAV_03 would be ready at slot 8. But MISSION_06 baseline depart 7, which is impossible because charging occupies slot 7. So we need to delay MISSION_06 depart to after charging finishes, slot 8. Due slot for MISSION_06 is 21, travel PATH_01 1 slot, arrival slot 9, fine. So shifting MISSION_06 to depart 8 is okay. But baseline had depart 7, so we need to adjust.\n\nThus for UAV_03, we need to: MISSION_03 depart later, charge some blocks, MISSION_06 depart later. Let's explore feasible plan.\n\nNow UAV_02: MISSION_07 baseline depart 3, PATH_09, energy 0.200697, initial SOC UAV_02 is 5.006967. Reserve 2.5. Required 0.200697 + 2.5 = 2.700697, SOC 5.006967 > 2.700697, ok. After mission SOC = 5.006967 - 0.200697 = 4.80627. No charging. Then MISSION_01 (seq1) origin VP_01, dest VP_02. Baseline depart 6, path PATH_07 (COR_07, 2 slots, energy 0.167248). But COR_07 is closed from slot 6 onward! Closed slots 6-43. So PATH_07 is not feasible. PATH_03 uses COR_04, which is closed from slots 6-17. Depart 6 + travel 2, so corridor used slots 6 and 7? Actually PATH_03 edge_segments: COR_04, duration 2, offset 0, so uses slots 6 and 7. COR_04 closed slots 6-17, so not feasible. PATH_08 uses COR_05, not closed, capacity 2, travel 14 slots, energy 1.337981. Depart 6, arrival 20. Due 23, so feasible. Energy: starting SOC 4.80627, required 1.337981 + 2.5 = 3.837981, so insufficient! SOC 4.80627 - 1.337981 = 3.468289, reserve check: 3.468289 >= 2.5, ok. But actually we need enough energy to do the mission: SOC must be >= service energy + reserve. 4.80627 > 3.837981? 4.80627 is greater than 3.837981, yes it is greater. Wait 4.80627 > 3.837981, so it's sufficient. So energy is fine. But PATH_08 is long, arrival 20. Then MISSION_04 (seq2) baseline depart 22, path PATH_02 (COR_08, 1 slot, energy 1.337981). Origin VP_02, dest VP_04. MISSION_01 arrives at VP_02 at slot 20. Turnaround 1 slot (slot 20), so available at VP_02 from slot 21. So MISSION_04 can depart at 22 as baseline. That works. Energy: after MISSION_01 SOC 3.468289. MISSION_04 energy 1.337981, reserve 2.5, required 3.837981, SOC 3.468289 < 3.837981, so not enough! Need to charge after MISSION_01. So we need charge_blocks for MISSION_01. Charging at VP_02: MISSION_01 arrives at 20, turnaround slot 20, charging starts slot 21. Charge blocks add 0.95 each. To get SOC from 3.468289 to at least 3.837981, need (3.837981 - 3.468289) = 0.369692 kWh, so 1 block (0.95) is enough. So charge_blocks >=1. But max 3, could charge more to help later. Charging adds time, so MISSION_04 must depart after charging. If charge 1 block, charging slot 21, then ready at slot 22, so depart 22 is ok. So baseline depart 22 works.\n\nBut MISSION_01 depart slot 6 with PATH_08 is possible as long as no corridor conflict. COR_05 capacity 2, no closures, so ok. But wait, we need to check MISSION_07 arrival and pad occupancy at VP_01. MISSION_07 arrives at VP_01 at slot 4 (depart 3 + 1). Turnaround 1 slot (slot 4). Then charging? Baseline had charge_blocks 0, so no charging. So MISSION_07 finishes occupation at VP_01 at end of slot 4? After turnaround, UAV_02 is at VP_01, ready for next mission at slot 5. But MISSION_01 origin is VP_01, so UAV_02 must be at VP_01 to depart at slot 6. That's fine. Pad capacity at VP_01: MISSION_07 occupied pad during slot 4 (turnaround). No conflict with other UAVs at VP_01 at that time? UAV_03 arrives at VP_01 at slot 6 (if depart 4, arrival 6). So no overlap.\n\nNow UAV_01: MISSION_02 baseline depart 20, path PATH_06 (COR_09, 2 slots, energy 0.12543). Slot 20 departure, arrival 22. COR_09 capacity 2, no closures, ok. Energy: UAV_01 initial SOC 7.5, reserve 1.5, required 0.12543+1.5=1.62543, plenty. SOC after = 7.5-0.12543=7.37457. No charging. MISSION_05 depart 24, path PATH_10 (COR_10, 1 slot, energy 0.08814). Origin VP_03, dest VP_05. MISSION_02 destination VP_03, arrival 22. Turnaround slot 22, then ready at slot 23. So depart 24 is fine. Energy: after MISSION_02 SOC 7.37457, MISSION_05 energy 0.08814, reserve 1.5, required 1.58814, ok. SOC after = 7.37457-0.08814=7.28643. No charging needed. So UAV_01 baseline is feasible, no closure issues.\n\nNow back to UAV_03 and UAV_02 conflict at slot 3 COR_01. We need to adjust. Possible options: shift MISSION_07 later, or MISSION_03 later, or change path. MISSION_07 has only PATH_09, can't change path. MISSION_03 has only PATH_04 fast enough (due slot 9), so cannot change path. So we need to serialize them on COR_01. COR_01 capacity at slot 3 is 1, so only one can use it at slot 3. If we keep MISSION_07 at slot 3, then MISSION_03 must not use slot 3. MISSION_03 path PATH_04 uses slots 3 and 4. If we delay MISSION_03 to